NAME
       xfs_growfs - grow a mounted XFS file system into new space

SYNOPSIS
       xfs_growfs [-d] [-D SIZE] [-n] MOUNT-POINT

DESCRIPTION
       After the device under an XFS file system has grown (usually a logical
       volume after lvextend), the file system still has its old size.
       xfs_growfs makes it use the new space. The file system must be
       mounted, and it keeps working while it grows: nothing has to stop.

       XFS can only grow. There is no way to shrink an XFS file system: to
       make one smaller, back up the data, make a new, smaller file system
       and restore. ext4 file systems can grow and (unmounted) shrink with
       resize2fs.

       Name the mount point. Newer versions also accept the device, but the
       mount point always works.

       lvextend -r (--resizefs) grows the LV and runs xfs_growfs (or
       resize2fs for ext4) in one step, which is the easiest way.

OPTIONS
       -d     Grow the data section to the full size of the device (the
              default when no option is given).

       -D SIZE
              Grow to SIZE file system blocks instead of the whole device.

       -n     Do not change anything; print the file system's geometry (like
              xfs_info).

EXAMPLES
       Grow the LV lvdata by 500 MiB and then its XFS file system:

           lvextend -L +500M /dev/vgdata/lvdata
           xfs_growfs /mnt/data
           df -h /mnt/data

       The same in one step:

           lvextend -r -L +500M /dev/vgdata/lvdata

       Grow it to a total of 2 GiB, extending the VG first if it is full:

           vgextend vgdata /dev/vdc1
           lvextend -r -L 2G /dev/vgdata/lvdata

NOTES
       If df still shows the old size, the file system was not grown: run
       xfs_growfs on the mount point.

       lvextend -L 2G sets the size to 2 GiB; lvextend -L +2G adds 2 GiB.

SEE ALSO
       lvextend(8), xfs_info(8), resize2fs(8), df(1), mkfs.xfs(8)
